add('K64CriticC1Predicate', 'critic', `Attack C1's frozen predicate and counter as a forger would.
Read ${ROUTE.plan} C1 and ${ROUTE.c1probe}. Find the cheapest way to read RECEIPT64_HELD 5/5 at a revision where the claimed work was not done or is fake: a tool that special-cases the two real report commits; a tool whose --selftest prints the PASS line without running cases; controls whose expect files were written to match a broken tool; P4's stub harness running the same harness the real tool does (what if the real tool were a stub that detects it is being run from the probe's temp dir?); P5 passing for a report that was written to satisfy the tool. For each attack write the reproducing fixture under ${OUT('K64CriticC1Predicate')}/ and run the frozen counter on it (git-archive a scratch commit; sandbox clone only, never the lane). Verdict per attack: HELD-FORGED (counter accepts) or REFUSED, with the reproducing command. Also judge the do-nothing story: is a do-nothing lane really at 0/5, and is 5/5 reachable by a lane that did the claimed work honestly?`, ['calibration'], 'attack C1 counter');

add('K64CriticC2Predicate', 'critic', `Attack C2's frozen predicate and counter as a forger would.
Read ${ROUTE.plan} C2 and ${ROUTE.c2probe}, ${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/probe/jj-donothing-control.sh and ${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/base/jj64-donothing.txt (do-nothing reads 1/8). Find the cheapest path to JJ64_HELD 8/8 that skips the claimed work: a demo script that simply echoes the entry's stated values (J6), an attacker-hosted row URL (J5), rows citing the right documents with different pages, a located quote copied from a different part of the page, an amendment that adds only the heading words, an entry that is the staged text with provenance retyped. For each attack build the fixture in a sandbox clone under ${OUT('K64CriticC2Predicate')}/ (D=$(mktemp -d) && git -C "$D" init -q style clones, explicit -C everywhere) and run the frozen counter; report HELD-FORGED or REFUSED with the reproducing command, and say which forgeries are inherent to a counter that measures an evidence chain.`, ['calibration'], 'attack C2 counter');

add('K64ReviewProbeC1', 'reviewer', `Code review of the frozen counter ${ROUTE.c1probe} and its control writer ${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/probe/make-receipt-controls.mjs.
Look for: shell/path injection through lane or rev arguments, temp-dir leaks, git archive failures swallowed, P2's case enumeration (cases without expect silently skipped; extra cases that lower the bar), P3's fleet blob reads (what if the fleet commit ids 7b0581ed/8fd46e61 are ambiguous or unreachable after a gc), exit-code mapping, P4's stubs run in the same temp dir as the tool, anything that makes the counter non-deterministic or host-dependent (HOME, network, clock). For each defect give severity, a reproducing command, and a minimal diff. Do not edit the counter.`, ['operability'], 'review C1 counter');

add('K64ReviewProbeC2', 'reviewer', `Code review of the frozen counter ${ROUTE.c2probe} (adapted from the round-63 topology counter) and the do-nothing control script.
Check each of J1..J8 against the plan text (${ROUTE.plan}) for any sentence/code mismatch (the plan and the counter must say the same thing); the 'bullets.length < 4' change vs the plan; the J6 parser (key regex [a-z0-9_]+, values containing '=', trailing spaces, duplicate keys), J6 timeout, the demo run inside an archive dir, J8's BASE constant and its prefix test (CRLF, a trailing newline difference), J7's three-way conjunction, live-fetch behaviour with redirects/403, curl failure vs MISS. Reproduce each defect with a command. Do not edit the counter.`, ['operability'], 'review C2 counter');

add('K64ReviewTool', 'reviewer', `Code review of ${ROUTE.tool}.
Read all of it. Look for: crashes on empty/binary/huge input; RegExp state bugs (global regex .test with lastIndex; matchAll on a non-global regex); git argument injection via --lane/--rev/--report (a rev beginning with '-'); ReDoS in the label/receipt/hash patterns; line-match logic in TALLY (line() false-accepts when the label occurs and x/y occurs elsewhere on the same line: build the case); receipt reading via git show of every blob in base/ and close/ (size, binary, symlink); worktree mode versus rev mode divergence; the 'tip' fallback when rev is absent and the lane has no HEAD; the fleet hash check using ^{commit} (a tree/blob hash is HASH_DEAD?); exit codes; the selftest leaving fixture dirs on failure. For each: severity, reproducing command, minimal diff. Do not edit the tool.`, ['operability'], 'review receipt tool');

add('K64ReviewDemo', 'reviewer', `Review ${ROUTE.demo} for hermeticity and honesty of what it claims to measure.
Questions: does any call touch the real lane or a real jj repo (every J call names -R; does 'jj git init' inside (cd "$D") read parent configuration?); does JJ_CONFIG=/dev/null suppress user config entirely or only the file (check jj docs/behaviour: env JJ_CONFIG semantics, repo and workspace config, JJ_USER?); is each outcome key's derivation sound (grep -c on status text: locale, wording of the 'stale' message, color/pager; the 'seat_snapshot_in_shared_op_log' count of 2 — what exactly does it count and is it stable across jj versions?); does 'rewrite' really rewrite the seat's @ (look at change id vs commit id; prove with jj log output) and does 'abandon' behave the same when the seat has NO uncommitted file (a variant not in the demo); is 'absolute_path_write_seen_in_default' proving containment failure or just default-workspace snapshot? Reproduce with commands in a fresh mktemp fixture (D=$(mktemp -d); explicit -R everywhere; never in the lane). Report defects with minimal diffs.`, ['depth', 'operability'], 'review jj demo');

add('K64SentinelDemoHost', 'sentinel', `Adversarial host-safety review of the demo and both counters: could running them harm or leak from the host?
Read ${ROUTE.demo}, ${ROUTE.c1probe}, ${ROUTE.c2probe}, ${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/probe/jj-donothing-control.sh. Check: rm -rf in the EXIT trap with an unset/empty variable or a mktemp failure (set -u? D and S empty -> 'rm -rf "" ""'), a TMPDIR set to a path that makes $D equal a parent dir, writing outside the fixtures, reading the operator's ~/.config/jj or ~/.gitconfig (signing keys, hooks via core.hooksPath, include paths) and executing hooks from them, network access (curl in J5) leaking nothing sensitive (UA string, no credentials), the do-nothing control cloning the lane with --no-hardlinks and committing inside the clone only (verify git -C "$C" rev-parse --show-toplevel guard), the counters exec'ing archive content (a malicious scripts/*.mjs in the measured revision runs with the operator's privileges: state it as the standing trust boundary and whether bwrap/cap-run could bound it). Reproduce each finding with a command in a mktemp fixture; rank by severity.`, ['operability'], 'host safety of demo/counters');

add('K64MutateProbes', 'mutation-tester', `Mutation-test BOTH frozen counters: ${ROUTE.c1probe} and ${ROUTE.c2probe}.
Copy each into ${OUT('K64MutateProbes')}/work/ and apply single-literal mutants one at a time (>= 30 per counter: every threshold N >= 20, cases < 14, ids < 8, clean < 2, bullets < 4, stated < 12, each regex, each check's boolean, each exit code, the P4 stub verdicts, the BASE constant, the OPENED date). For C1 the harness needs a measured revision: use a sandbox clone of the lane (git clone --no-hardlinks; commit inside the clone only with -c user.name=fixture -c user.email=fixture@example.invalid; verify git -C "$C" rev-parse --show-toplevel) at ${FREEZE}; since the counter reads UNMET there anyway, build a 'tool-present' revision by also copying the tool+controls from ${FREEZE} — they are already committed there, so run the unmutated counter at ${FREEZE} first to record the honest score (expect P1..P4 pass, P5 miss without --report) and judge each mutant by whether the verdict line changes. For C2 run against the do-nothing clone and against the honest fixture if K64ExecJjHonestFixture's path exists. A mutant whose verdict line equals the unmutated line is a SURVIVOR: classify equivalent (reason) or write the missing control (as a described fixture; never edit the frozen counter). Table: id, mutation, changed-verdict?, survivor-verdict.`, ['calibration'], 'mutate counters');

add('K64ReviewPlanText', 'reviewer', `Read the frozen plan ${ROUTE.plan} against the counters, the tool and the repository state, as the closure reviewer will.
Check every sentence that can be checked: the counts claimed (16 controls; 15 demo keys; 136 rows; collision list S195/S196/S197; 'readme-map DRIFT'/'proxy-check DRIFT' at base), the cited commit hashes and paths (git cat-file), each plan sentence of C1/C2 against the code of the counter that scores it (plan and code must say the same thing: AXES.md and the addendum say the predicate is one sentence identical in plan, code and label), the do-nothing story, the 'stated limit' paragraphs, and whether any 'all/every/complete' clause lacks an enumerating command (addendum §9). List each mismatch with file:line on both sides. A plan defect found NOW is disclosed (append-only CHANGES-AFTER-FREEZE), so be exact.`, ['calibration'], 'review frozen plan');

